WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
This one I just really want to put out there:

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:08.160
An overview on the language of filmmaking, focusing on the basic cuts.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.940
[music]

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.060
This is going to be a very broad overview of the basics of the subject

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.400
Primarily aimed to people who are new.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.500
If you're already familiar with the language of editing, then this may be mostly repetition of stuff you already know.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.080
But I hope I've managed to present the information in a way that's entertaining,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.760
and maybe makes you take a second look at the way that you think about what you already know.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.940
The first thing that needs to be mentally sorted is that there are two broad categories of cuts that's we're going to talk about.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:48.520
Or, put another way, there are two different contexts where we'll talk about the type of cut used.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.420
The first is 'Mechanical:'

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.040
What - physically - is the Editor doing to assemble the footage?

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.980
The second is 'Narrative:'

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.280
What is the outcome of the cut in terms of story and meaning?

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.160
It's important to be aware of these two different contexts

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.680
because it helps keep in mind that a cut is often more than just one thing,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.740
usually having at least two components -  its physical form and its narrative function.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.920
First, the Mechanical Cuts:

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.320
Right off the top, the single most common type of cut,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.780
'The Straight Cut.'

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.060
One clip ends.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.560
Another begins.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.600
This is the default cut.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.900
This is what you get when you take two different shots and join them together: a straight cut.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.680
Next, the Split Edit.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.400
Now, these days, this applies more cleanly to documentary, corporate video, and commercials,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.860
simply because of the way that narrative features are assembled.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.440
The Split Edit comes in two flavors:

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.440
The L cut and the J cut.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.820
The whole idea here is that the audio associated with a clip either leads or trails the cut.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.480
When the audio leads the cut, it's a J-cut,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.980
and when the audio trails, it's an L-cut.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.400
The reason for the naming is visual.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.580
In the editing suite, the cuts end up looking like this:

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:06.660
L

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.580
and J

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:12.020
In a narrative feature, this gets...muddled from moment to moment.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.540
The editing in a competent film can get quite complicated--

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:21.520
Particularly because the soundtrack is typically woven together from dozens of different sources.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.440
Take, for example, this dialogue in 10 Cloverfield Lane

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:29.720
where the sound and picture weaves back and forth between the two characters.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.820
You can't leave

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.760
An attack means fallout

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.080
Which contaminates the air above ground. That's how it works.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.540
Well how do we have to wait until its safe?

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.880
Depends on the proximity of the closest blast. One year, maybe two.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.520
[loudly whispered] 10 Cloverfield Lane is a very good movie and its editing is excellent.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.200
Because of this complexity, the place that a split edit is going to be MOST clear is over a scene transition,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.680
where audio from a different time and place leads or trails the cut.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:11.860
This might be substantial such as this moment in 'Man of Steel,' where the incoming audio leads the cut by several seconds:

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.800
YOUNG CLARK: I'm tired of safe!

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.180
YOUNG CLARK: I want to do something useful with my life.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:25.780
Or it might be quite subtle like the sound of this door closing trailing the cut by a matter of frames

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.520
Retain, even in opposition,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.160
Your capacity for astonishment.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.100
[door shuts]

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.660
Next up is the 'Jump Cut.'

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:45.500
This one, you're probably intensely familiar with, as it has been used,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.360
some would say, to excess,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.360
on YouTube.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.240
This is when a clip cuts into itself.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.840
Now, there's another use of this phrase, as a pejorative,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.980
And that's when a clip cuts into a second clip that

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.100
isn't properly differentiated from the first clip.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.580
Like, they may technically be two different shots, but

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.880
they're too similar, making the cut between them feel

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.400
awkward and wrong.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.660
The last of the mechanical cuts is the 'Transition.'

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.200
A Transition cut is any kind of mixing effect that merges two clips together,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.900
whether it be a fade, a dissolve or any of the many many MANY wipe or transit effects,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.560
Including, but not limited to:

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.020
Linear horizontal.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.400
Push horizontal

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.580
Linear vertical.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:32.880
Push vertical.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.100
Barn doors.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.120
Iris.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.340
Crosswipe.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.380
Star-wipe.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.560
Heart-wipe.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:39.820
Diamond-wipe.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.500
Radial. Wipe.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:42.900
Card-wipe.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.920
Venetian blinds.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.460
Twister.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.500
Jaaws

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.940
Cube spin.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:50.760
Flipover

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.080
Page peel.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.900
Page tuurn

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.780
And cross-zoom.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.560
Moving on, we get into the narrative cuts, describing a cut in terms of its intent or content.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.720
This is also where things start to get really funky because any given cut may function as one or more of these.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.640
For example, let's start with the 'Match Cut.'

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:18.980
The basic idea of a match cut is that some visual element is present to make physically discontinuous action

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.840
appear continuous.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.340
The hand starts to raise, in the A-shot,

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:28.060
And the action is completed in the B-shot

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:31.560
This idea expands into the 'Graphic Match Cut,'

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.840
where some visual element on each side of the cut links the two together

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.100
Such as this, the best edit in all of 'The Lost World", Jurassic Park

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:47.460
[man shouts at another person]
[music swells]

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.840
[woman screams]

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.300
[blends into screech of a subway]

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.500
[screeching subway]

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:04.260
In this case, in addition to the visual match of the mouths, the audio of the scream is also blended with the sound of the train.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.160
So this is a match both visually and on the soundtrack

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.940
This specific cut is also an example of a Contrast Cut;

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:17.300
The mother's terror is juxtaposed with Malcolm's boredom to create humor

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.520
At least I think that's what they were going for?

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.860
It's - it's a very strange edit.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.340
The Contrast cut can also be used with the intent of drawing a connection between two things,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.700
to imply they are an extension of one another or to create commentary.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.500
Cutting from a crowd of Christmas shoppers to a herd of sheep being the archetypal example.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.500
Hey, hey Dan.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.300
Do you know about that one shot?

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.380
They do that in 2001.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:49.660
You know, that shot with the bone and the bone becomes a missile platform

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.860
You know about that shot, right?

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:54.020
Yeah, I know about that shot.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:55.160
ok

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.500
[whispered] JUST CHECKING.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.920
The next that we're going to discuss is the 'Crosscut.'

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:10.940
Now, cross-cutting, also called parallel cutting, isn't quite accurate as a singular cut,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.020
but is rather a description of a series of cuts.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:18.480
A way of structuring a scene where two displaced actions are cut together,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.940
the implication being that they are happening simultaneously, or near simultaneously.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.580
This is fairly standard for action scenes,

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:32.760
where there may not be anything other than general implication or location to tie shots to each other.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:39.820
Such as in the bike chase in Akira where we cut between various aspects of the chase without strict continuity of action.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:40.720
[grits teeth noise]

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.000
[also gritting teeth noise]
[wind whipping by]

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:45.700
uahh!

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.860
oaah!

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.940
[groans]

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:55.100
In the film, 'In Bruges,' it's used to connect three simultaneous events.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.480
Ken, crawling to the window of the bell tower;

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.680
Harry, descending the bell tower;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.140
and Ray at the cafe with Chloe.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.260
Three events; three locations,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.100
Intercut to keep the audience oriented to things that are happening simultaneously.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.960
The last type of edit that we're gonna talk about is a bit of a special consideration

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.700
since it describes a kind of shot as much as it describes a kind of edit.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.540
And that's the 'Insert.'

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:25.240
An Insert is a cut from the main action to a detail of interest.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.720
Either to the material of the story or to the thematic fabric.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:36.360
A common example of an insert is basically anytime a character is doing something with their hands.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.440
In documentaries, inserts are used frequently to simply add interest and rhythm -

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.120
to illustrate a point or to facilitate pacing.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.360
From 'Enron: The Smartest Guys in the Room,'

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:53.620
we have all of these shots of documents being shredded while the narrator talks about Enron shredding documents.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:09:00.480
But the important thing to keep in mind is that this is not actually footage of Enron documents being shredded.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.680
This is not literally what they're talking about - it's just symbolic of what they're talking about.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:08.720
So there you go:

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.440
a broad overview of the basic Lexicon of editing.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:13.660
Here's a kitty.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:23.380
[upbeat music]

